  <section class="ac-section">
    <h2>Ohio Overtime Formulas &amp; 2026 Payroll Withholding Rules</h2>
    <p>
      Ohio follows the federal Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) 40-hour workweek standard. Unlike California, working a 10-hour or 12-hour shift does not automatically trigger overtime in Ohio unless your cumulative hours exceed 40 in that seven-day workweek.
    </p>
    <div class="ac-formula">
Overtime Hourly Rate ($)  = Base Hourly Rate × 1.5
Double-Time Rate ($)      = Base Hourly Rate × 2.0 (contract/holiday policy)
Weekly Gross ($)          = (Reg Hours × Base Rate) + (OT Hours × 1.5 × Base Rate) + (DT Hours × 2.0 × Base Rate)

Incremental Net OT ($)    = Net Pay (With Overtime) − Net Pay (40 Regular Hours Only)
Net Take-Home per OT Hour = Incremental Net OT ($) ÷ Total Overtime Hours Worked
    </div>
    <h3>Why Overtime Paychecks Seem Taxed Heavier (The Annualization Effect)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No Special "Overtime Tax Rate":</strong> Overtime wages are taxed at the exact same ordinary income brackets as regular wages when you file your return.</li>
      <li><strong>Why Withholding Jumps Temporarily:</strong> Payroll software calculates each paycheck by assuming you earn that inflated amount every single week of the year (×52). A single 55-hour week can push that week's federal withholding from the 12% bracket into the 22% bracket—any over-withholding is refunded when you file your IRS Form 1040.</li>
      <li><strong>2026 Ohio Flat Tax Simplifies OT:</strong> Because Ohio's 2026 state rate is a flat <strong>2.75%</strong> on all taxable income over $26,050, working overtime does not push you into a higher Ohio state bracket.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

