  <section class="ac-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Ohio Overtime Pay</h2>
    <div class="ac-faq-list">
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>1. Does Ohio require overtime pay after 8 hours in a single day?</h3>
        <p>No. Under Ohio Revised Code §4111.03, overtime is calculated strictly on a <strong>40-hour workweek</strong> basis. You can work four 10-hour shifts (40 hours total) without earning statutory overtime unless a union contract or employer handbook provides daily overtime.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>2. Can working overtime actually make my paycheck smaller?</h3>
        <p>No—working overtime always increases your net pay. US federal brackets are marginal, meaning higher tax rates only apply to the dollars inside the higher bracket, and Ohio's 2026 state rate is a flat 2.75% above $26,050. You never lose money by working overtime.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>3. Are Ohio municipal city taxes withheld on overtime pay?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Overtime and double-time pay are treated as qualifying wages for Ohio municipal income taxes (2.5% in Columbus, Cleveland, Akron, Toledo, Dayton; 1.8% in Cincinnati).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>4. Does PTO or holiday pay count toward the 40-hour overtime threshold in Ohio?</h3>
        <p>Under Ohio and federal FLSA law, only <strong>hours actually worked</strong> count toward the 40-hour threshold. If you take 8 hours of paid vacation Monday and work 36 hours Tuesday–Friday (44 paid hours total), your employer is not legally required to pay overtime unless company policy states otherwise.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>5. Is double-time pay required on Sundays or holidays in Ohio?</h3>
        <p>No. Neither Ohio law nor the federal FLSA requires double-time (2.0×) pay for weekends or holidays. Double-time is strictly an employer benefit or collective-bargaining union provision.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
